    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d511">

                <pb facs="0457" n="341" sameAs="frus:frus1914/pg341"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 838.00/893.</note>
                <head ana="#head_untitled-inferred-from-preceding-sibling-head">[Untitled]</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Legation</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Port au Prince</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1914-03-19">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">March 19, 1914</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>The following telegram has been received from Consul Livingston: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>Revolution receiving support on the frontier from Dominican side and may
                            be prolonged. Overtures made to me by a friend of Theodore and by
                            Minister Charles Zamor to attempt an arrangement. Please ask permission
                            Department of State for me, with cooperation Commander of <hi
                                rendition="#italic">Nashville</hi>, to proceed to Dejabon and
                            Ouanaminthe via Monte Cristi.</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <p>I remarked that this work be attended in perfect accord [sic] as Livingston
                    suggests, as the situation mentioned needs attention.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Smith</hi></signed>.</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
